---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../../../styles/story.css";
import ReadingModes from "../../../components/ReadingModes.astro";
import Cite from "../../../components/Cite.astro";
import DataTable from "../../../components/story/DataTable.astro";
import DieselChart from "../../../components/DieselChart.astro";
import StorySources from "../../../components/StorySources.astro";
import StoryState from "../../../components/StoryState.astro";
import StateEvent from "../../../components/StateEvent.astro";
import { readPublishedState, eventPath } from "../../../lib/state.mjs";
import { event, prices, release, marchSoFar, shortfalls, usShare, next, ledgerRows } from "../../../data/fuel-reserves-2026-10-02-g7-releases-100-million-barrels.mjs";
const state = readPublishedState().events.get("event-fuel-reserves-2026-10-02-g7-releases-100-million-barrels");
if (!state) throw new Error("Missing publication selection: event-fuel-reserves-2026-10-02-g7-releases-100-million-barrels");
if (state.status === "merged") return Astro.redirect(eventPath(state.survivor), 301);
const markers = [
  { date: "2026-02-28", label: "War with Iran", record: "euronews-eu-document-2026-10-08", passage: "attacks against Iran on 28 February" },
  { peak: true, anchor: "end" },
  { date: "2026-10-02", label: "G7 release", anchor: "end", record: "govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02", passage: "coordinated release through the IEA of 100 million barrels (MB) to begin immediately over 4 months" },
];
---
{state.status === "retired" ? <StateEvent view={state} /> : <BaseLayout title={`${event.title} | The Catch`} current="Events">
  <div slot="locator" class="locator"><a href="/events/">Series</a><a href="/events/fuel-reserves/">Fuel reserves</a><span>October 2, 2026</span></div>
  <div class="content">
    <main class="story" id="story-root">
      <p class="story-kicker" data-layer="fact">Fuel reserves &middot; October 2, 2026 &middot; updated {event.updatedLabel}</p>
      <h1 data-layer="fact">{event.title}</h1>
      <p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">{event.dek}</p>
      <ReadingModes />
      <nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story" data-layer="fact">
        <details class="toc-disclosure" open><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li>
          <li><a href="#diesel">Diesel since the war</a></li>
          <li><a href="#owed">Who still owes barrels</a></li>
          <li><a href="#washington">Washington's push</a></li>
          <li><a href="#sides">The sides</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-catch">The catch</a></li>
          <li><a href="#next">What happens next</a></li>
          <li><a href="#records">The records</a></li>
        </ol>
        </details>
      </nav>

      <section id="what-happened">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="1">The leaders of the G7 agreed on October 2 to release {release.barrels} barrels of oil from government emergency stocks over {release.months}, with a large release of diesel in the first {release.dieselDays}. The barrels were already promised. The G7's statement presents them as carrying out a pledge governments made in March, after the war with Iran began, to release {release.marchTotal} barrels, and about {marchSoFar.unreleased} barrels of that pledge are still unreleased.<Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="coordinated release through the IEA of 100 million barrels (MB) to begin immediately over 4 months, including a frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="taking into account commitments that have already been fulfilled, we will implement our commitments" /><Cite s="doe-spr-release-2026-03-11" passage="coordinated release of 400 million barrels" /><Cite s="iea-member-meeting-2026-10-07" passage="would bring approximately 100 million barrels to the market" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="4">The White House tells it differently. It says Trump negotiated {release.barrels} barrels of diesel from Europe. The G7 text gives no split between crude and diesel, and it has G7 members and partners releasing, not Europe alone.<Cite s="whitehouse-diesel-fact-sheet-2026-10-05" passage="President Trump successfully negotiated with Europe to release 100 million barrels of refined diesel" /><Cite s="reuters-iea-board-2026-10-06" passage="They did not provide a breakdown of the volumes of crude, diesel and other products to be released" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="within the first 20 days by G7 members and partners" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">What the agreement adds is speed: the agency's member governments have backed finishing the March release as soon as possible, diesel first where they can. Germany says it will add nothing beyond March for now, and France has floated {shortfalls.franceExtra} of diesel without a firm offer. JPMorgan's analysts said the headline {release.barrels} "does not represent 100 million barrels of new intervention."<Cite s="iea-member-meeting-2026-10-07" passage="IEA Members also supported the prioritisation of the release of diesel stocks, to the extent possible" /><Cite s="iea-member-meeting-2026-10-07" passage="accelerating the oil stock releases announced in the Collective Action of March 2026" /><Cite s="politico-eu-france-germany-2026-10-07" passage="as of now, no additional quantities" /><Cite s="politico-eu-france-germany-2026-10-07" passage="France on Wednesday signaled it could release 2 million barrels of diesel on top of the quantities agreed in March" /><Cite s="reuters-eu-march-volumes-2026-10-07" passage="the headline 100 million barrels does not represent 100 million barrels of new intervention" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="2">Diesel is in tight supply. In the U.S. it has gone from {prices.beforeWar} to {prices.oct5} a gallon since the war began.<Cite s="iea-member-meeting-2026-10-07" passage="given the current tightness in diesel markets" /><Cite s="eia-diesel-price-2026-10-06" passage="02/23 3.809" /><Cite s="eia-diesel-price-2026-10-06" passage="2026-Oct 10/05 6.199" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="3">Washington pressed Europe to release its stocks faster, saying it had delivered only a fraction of its pledge, and Trump weighed banning U.S. diesel exports, which Europe relies on.<Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="several European member countries have released only a fraction of the crude oil and petroleum products they pledged" /><Cite s="cnbc-export-ban-2026-09-30" passage='still "thinking about" banning diesel exports' /><Cite s="national-g7-release-2026-10-02" passage="The IEA said the US supplied roughly half of the EU's diesel imports in August." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="5">The International Energy Agency, which coordinates the emergency stocks of {release.members} countries, holds a board meeting next week that is expected to settle who releases what, and how much of it is diesel.<Cite s="doe-spr-release-2026-03-11" passage="32 member nations of the International Energy Agency" /><Cite s="iea-member-meeting-2026-10-07" passage="the next scheduled meeting of the IEA Governing Board, which will take place next week" /><Cite s="reuters-iea-board-2026-10-06" passage="is expected to decide the details of the stocks release at an October 14-15 board meeting" /><Cite s="euronews-eu-document-2026-10-08" passage="including volumes, timing and the type of refined oil products to be released" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="diesel">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Diesel since the war</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="2">The United States and Israel attacked Iran on February 28, and tanker traffic through the Strait of Hormuz, the Gulf's sea route to the world, nearly stopped.<Cite s="euronews-eu-document-2026-10-08" passage="attacks against Iran on 28 February and the closure of the Strait of Hormuz" /> On March 11 the agency's members pledged to release {release.marchTotal} barrels, {release.usMarch} of them from the U.S. Strategic Petroleum Reserve.<Cite s="doe-spr-release-2026-03-11" passage="coordinated release of 400 million barrels" /><Cite s="doe-spr-release-2026-03-11" passage="release 172 million barrels from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Tankers found other routes, and crude exports from the Middle East recovered. Fuel stayed scarce: in August the Gulf's net diesel exports were about a quarter of their prewar level, and Ukrainian attacks on Russian refineries had nearly halted Russia's fuel exports.<Cite s="iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02" passage="While crude oil exports from the Middle East have recovered significantly" /><Cite s="iea-omr-2026-09" passage="averaged 390 kb/d in August, just over a quarter of pre-war levels" /><Cite s="iea-omr-2026-09" passage="a near-halt to product exports following intensified Ukrainian attacks" /> U.S. diesel went from {prices.beforeWar} a gallon the week before the war to a record {prices.record} on September 21, and was {prices.oct5} on October 5. In Europe diesel prices have doubled since late February.<Cite s="eia-diesel-price-2026-10-06" passage="02/23 3.809" /><Cite s="eia-diesel-price-2026-10-06" passage="09/21 6.529" /><Cite s="eia-diesel-price-2026-10-06" passage="2026-Oct 10/05 6.199" /><Cite s="xinhua-itkonen-2026-10-02" passage="diesel prices have doubled" /></p>
        <DieselChart source="eia-diesel-price-2026-10-06" from="2026-01-05" to="2026-10-05" markers={markers} />
      </section>

      <section id="owed">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Who still owes barrels</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">By October 2 about {marchSoFar.released} of the March barrels had been released. That would leave {marchSoFar.onPaper}, but some countries released more than they promised, and the countries that fell short still owe about {marchSoFar.unreleased}.<Cite s="iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02" passage="around 325 million barrels of the IEA collective action announced on 11 March have so far been released" /><Cite s="iea-member-meeting-2026-10-07" passage="with some countries having released greater volumes than they initially pledged" /><Cite s="iea-member-meeting-2026-10-07" passage="would bring approximately 100 million barrels to the market" /> Germany has released {shortfalls.germanyShare} of the oil it promised, and France has {shortfalls.franceLeft} left.<Cite s="politico-eu-france-germany-2026-10-07" passage="Of the 2.65 million tons of oil, diesel and jet fuel promised, Germany has so far released only 600,000 tons onto the market." /><Cite s="politico-eu-france-germany-2026-10-07" passage="Paris has indicated privately that it still has 8 million barrels left under the March commitment" /> The United States owes part of its own share. It has agreed to lend oil companies {usShare.awarded} of its {release.usMarch} barrels, and on September 29 it offered the last {usShare.offer} again, after a June offer found takers for only about 500,000.<Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="collectively awarded more than 133 million barrels" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="an exchange of up to 40 million barrels of crude oil" /><Cite s="reuters-spr-limits-2026-09-29" passage="In June, the US offered to loan the last 40 million barrels from that agreement, but later that month revealed that companies only agreed to borrow about 500,000 barrels." /></p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>The March pledge and what is left of it</strong></figcaption>
          <DataTable columns={["What is counted", "Barrels"]} rows={ledgerRows} ariaLabel="The March pledge, what has been released, what is still owed and the U.S. share" />
          <p class="chart-source">The agency's counts of October 2 and 7; the U.S. lending is summed from the Energy Department's five award notices, March 20 to June 22.<Cite s="iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02" passage="around 325 million barrels of the IEA collective action announced on 11 March have so far been released" /><Cite s="iea-member-meeting-2026-10-07" passage="would bring approximately 100 million barrels to the market" /><Cite s="doe-spr-award-2026-03-20" passage="exchange of 45.2 million barrels" /><Cite s="doe-spr-award-2026-04-10" passage="8.5-million-barrels of crude oil" /><Cite s="doe-spr-award-2026-04-17" passage="exchange 26 million barrels" /><Cite s="doe-spr-award-2026-05-11" passage="approximately 53.3 million barrels" /><Cite s="doe-spr-award-2026-06-22" passage="500,000 barrels" /></p>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section id="washington">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Washington's push</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="3">A week after diesel hit its record, Washington turned on Europe. Energy Secretary Chris Wright said on September 29 that the United States and Japan were delivering on their pledges while several European countries had released "only a fraction" of theirs.<Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="several European member countries have released only a fraction of the crude oil and petroleum products they pledged" /> Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent asked Europe to speed up and add more, because American farmers, truckers and businesses "should not be left carrying the burden of a global diesel shortage."<Cite s="bessent-post-2026-10-01" passage="Our European partners should accelerate delivery on their existing commitments and make additional supplies immediately available" /><Cite s="bessent-post-2026-10-01" passage="should not be left carrying the burden of a global diesel shortage" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Trump said on September 30 that he was still "thinking about" banning diesel exports. A ban would hit the European Union, which got about half of its diesel imports from the United States in August.<Cite s="cnbc-export-ban-2026-09-30" passage='still "thinking about" banning diesel exports' /><Cite s="national-g7-release-2026-10-02" passage="The IEA said the US supplied roughly half of the EU's diesel imports in August." /> Three people close to the talks said the administration had told France and Germany to release diesel or face a possible U.S. export ban.<Cite s="reuters-diesel-demand-2026-10-01" passage="or face a potential US diesel export ban, said three people close to the discussions" /> President Emmanuel Macron of France, which holds the G7 presidency, had promised on September 18 to convene the leaders on oil stocks, and on October 2 he did.<Cite s="elysee-macron-2026-09-18" passage="je convoquerai un G7 pour avancer sur une meilleure coordination sur les niveaux de stock" /><Cite s="iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02" passage="At the invitation of President Emmanuel Macron of France, which holds the 2026 G7 Presidency" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="sides">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">The sides</h2>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">Washington</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Trump wrote on October 2 that Europe had agreed to release "a massive amount" of diesel, and Wright called the release European and "the majority diesel."<Cite s="trumpstruth-post-2026-10-02" passage="Europe has just agreed to release a massive amount of their heavily stocked Diesel Oil." /><Cite s="wright-post-2026-10-02" passage="The 100-million-barrel release announced earlier today by European nations" /><Cite s="wright-post-2026-10-02" passage="the majority diesel" /> Hours after the call Trump ruled out a diesel export ban, saying: "It was never really ever on the table, but what Europe did was a great thing."<Cite s="reuters-no-export-ban-2026-10-02" passage="he will not authorize an export ban on diesel, hours after G7 countries agreed to release diesel and crude oil from their emergency reserves." /><Cite s="politico-no-ban-2026-10-02" passage="It was never really ever on the table, but what Europe did was a great thing" /></p>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">Europe</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Germany's government says the {release.barrels} barrels carry out the March obligations, with "no additional quantities" for now, and that its own barrels went unreleased because the market did not want them.<Cite s="politico-eu-france-germany-2026-10-07" passage="the obligations are to be implemented through a coordinated release of 100 million barrels via the IEA" /><Cite s="politico-eu-france-germany-2026-10-07" passage="as of now, no additional quantities" /><Cite s="bundesregierung-energy-faq-2026-10-01" passage="Derzeit wird vom Markt keine weitere Freigabe für erforderlich gehalten." /> The European Commission said: "We fully reject any ban on diesel." Macron said Trump did not threaten a diesel embargo during the call.<Cite s="france24-g7-release-2026-10-02" passage="We fully reject any ban on diesel." /><Cite s="national-g7-release-2026-10-02" passage="Mr Macron said Mr Trump did not threaten to issue an embargo on US diesel" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Both sides back the same plan: the barrels go through the agency, with a large diesel release up front, and the G7 countries recommitted to not restricting energy exports to one another.<Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="refrain from export restrictions on energy and energy products between G7 countries" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="including a frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <h3>"100 million barrels of refined diesel"</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative">The White House's fact sheet of October 5 says Trump negotiated "100 million barrels of refined diesel" from Europe's reserves. The G7 statement names no amount of diesel, France's account of the call says refined products, including diesel, and crude, and the leaders gave no breakdown.<Cite s="whitehouse-diesel-fact-sheet-2026-10-05" passage="President Trump successfully negotiated with Europe to release 100 million barrels of refined diesel" /><Cite s="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" passage="notamment de diesel et de pétrole brut" /><Cite s="reuters-iea-board-2026-10-06" passage="They did not provide a breakdown of the volumes of crude, diesel and other products to be released" /></p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="next">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happens next</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="5">The board is expected to meet October 14 and 15 to settle the volumes, the timing and which fuels go out. The agency also owes the G7 a follow-up report.<Cite s="reuters-iea-board-2026-10-06" passage="is expected to decide the details of the stocks release at an October 14-15 board meeting" /><Cite s="euronews-eu-document-2026-10-08" passage="including volumes, timing and the type of refined oil products to be released" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="A follow-up report should be delivered before 20 days" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Supply is still at risk. {next.hormuzVessels} commodity ships crossed Hormuz on October 6, the fewest since July 23, and on October 8, with Hurricane Isaias approaching, producers shut in {next.gulfShutIn} of U.S. Gulf oil output.<Cite s="reuters-hormuz-transits-2026-10-08" passage="Seven commodity vessels passed through the strait on Tuesday, for the lowest figure since July 23" /><Cite s="reuters-oil-jumps-2026-10-08" passage="have shut in about 1.3 million barrels per day, or 62.9%, of current oil production as of Thursday" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="records">
      <StorySources event={event.slug} />
      <StoryState view={state} supporting />
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>}
